+All methods use the same pretrained checkpoint and differ only in how the state is updated, so no model is retrained. CUT3R~\cite{cut3r} applies the full update and TTT3R~\cite{ttt3r} scales it with its confidence gate $\beta_t$, and we report TTSA3R~\cite{ttsa3r} where its results are available. We compare three variants of DDD3R that correspond to the two problems measured in \cref{sec:analysis}. \dddconst{} sets $\alpha_\perp{=}\alpha_\parallel{=}0.5$ and so damps all directions equally, which isolates the effect of smaller updates. \dddbrake{} replaces the two coefficients with a single per-token factor $g_t = \operatorname{sigmoid}(-\tau\cos(\boldsymbol{\delta}_t,\boldsymbol{\delta}_{t-1}))$ with $\tau{=}1$ (the aggregate KITTI Odometry results used $\tau{=}2$), which shrinks an update when it repeats the previous one but cannot separate directions within it (\cref{app:brake_ortho}). \dddortho{} is the full rule of \cref{eq:ddd3r} with $\alpha_\perp{=}0.5$ and $\alpha_\parallel{=}0.05$, our default. A variant that adapts $\alpha_\parallel$ to each token's drift energy is reported in \cref{app:gamma,app:spectrum}.
 
 \paragraph{Benchmarks.}
 We evaluate relative pose on TUM~\cite{tum}, ScanNet~\cite{scannet}, Sintel~\cite{sintel} and KITTI~\cite{kitti}, video depth on KITTI, Bonn~\cite{bonn} and Sintel, and 3D reconstruction on 7-Scenes~\cite{7scenes}. We report ATE RMSE after Sim(3) alignment for pose, scale-and-shift-invariant absolute relative error for depth, and accuracy, completeness and normal consistency for reconstruction, with per-dataset protocols in \cref{app:setup}.
